00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:06.130
So this game,
"Gyakuten Othellonia,"

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:08.650
is a strategy app game.

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.620
The player's action is based on
the board game called Othello

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:15.490
or Reversi, and the player
put black and white pieces

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:17.560
alternatively.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.510
And what is unique
about this game

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:23.560
here is that each piece
has its own skills, which

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.340
are strategic components
to the battle.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.650
Actually, when you
start playing the game,

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:31.660
there are varieties
of play styles,

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:37.540
and the interest exploration
is required to master the game.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.700
So this title was
released three years ago,

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.870
and fortunately,
it's still growing.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:47.110
We have more than 24
million downloads so far,

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:51.460
and the service region
is expanding gradually.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.880
Of course, I hope in the future,
we can release it in the US

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:55.380
as well.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.970
So in Othellonia,
there's the concept

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.260
of decks and characters.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.800
So before starting
the battle, player

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:07.840
constructs deck,
which is composed

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.970
of 16 characters among
more than 3,000 options,

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:15.090
and each character
have its own skills,

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:18.690
and the players have to choose
consistent characters according

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:21.270
to their play styles.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:25.970
As you can imagine, the number
of the combination is extreme,

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:30.630
and it's very hard
for beginners to learn

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:33.870
how to build optimal decks.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.660
Another issue here is that
building an optimal deck

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:40.230
requires to learn effective
strategies through the process

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:41.520
of trial and error.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:47.450
So let me explain why
this is a problem.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:50.570
So generally speaking,
if the game is complex,

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:54.320
a game has complex features,
which can distract players

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.810
from continuing the
game, the churn rate

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:00.870
increases and impacts the
future revenue, right?

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:02.780
And for example,
there is an estimation

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:06.530
that just a few points decrease
in the 30-day churn rate

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:14.350
can increase the revenue by
20% to 30% in the future.

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:16.480
So back to Othellonia--
we actually

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:20.829
have a high churn rate,
so we have trouble here.

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:24.710
And this is partly because
the game is complex, and lacks

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:28.060
sufficient on-boarding
support, and therefore it's

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:33.180
hard for beginners to
construct an optimal deck.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:35.850
So also, there is
a problem that they

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:38.160
don't have a good
place for practice

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.130
in order to improve
their play skills.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:45.780
For instance, we only have two
battle options for beginners.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.230
One is NPC battle, which they
battle against a very, very

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:53.050
weak opponent; and the
other is a PvP battle,

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:56.970
in which they are frequently
matched against very

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:00.010
skilled or experienced players.

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:04.980
So we need a team opponent
whose play level is similar

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:08.570
to the beginner's.

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:11.540
So here's our approach.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:15.390
To support beginners, we
have provided two functions.

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:18.110
The first one is the deck
recommendation system,

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:21.470
which selects appropriate
characters from players' piece

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:22.510
box--

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:25.190
and we are using
association analysis

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:28.480
to provide a better
recommendation.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.430
The second one is the battle AI,
which we call Othellonia Dojo.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:36.470
And in these functions,
we have developed

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:40.020
AI that can play at any levels.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:43.920
Our deep neural network
is used for this AI.

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:46.680
Later, Ikki will explain
how these techniques

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:47.790
are used in our cases.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:55.580
OK, so by the way, from the
machine learning perspective,

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.800
we need to provide--

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:01.250
prepare data to build such AIs.

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:04.760
We're correcting various
kinds of in-game data,

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:06.710
since the early
days of the game,

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:09.590
such as deck logs
and battle logs,

00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:15.200
so we had a good amount of data
when we started this project.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.110
And in our cases,
we used the data

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:20.540
for the last nine
months, which contains

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:23.890
billions of log entries.

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:26.960
Then we filtered the
data by player skills,

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:32.670
and augmented the original
data in the battle AI cases.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:36.390
The image on the right
side is an example

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:40.350
of battle logs, which
is formatted in JSON,

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:43.170
and describes entire
state and action

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:46.310
information of the battle.

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:49.370
And the bottom diagram
shows rough data pipelines,

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:54.050
and BigQuery did a very good job
at this pre-processing phase.

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:58.070
For instance, it can handle
even terabytes order of data

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:01.610
very fast, and we could
reduce the processing time

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:03.370
drastically.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:07.470
BQ could handle our data
in a few minutes and--

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:12.630
well, compared with on-prem
Hadoop clusters that took,

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:14.460
like, an hour.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:17.200
So yeah, we are very,
very happy here.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.300
And we also love that BQ is easy
to corroborate with another GCP

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.290
services.

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:26.630
And here I want to stress that
the aria has been successfully

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:30.780
built by sufficient amount of
data and a reliable pipeline.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:38.040
So let's see how each of
these use cases works.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:42.180
This is a rough overview of
the deck recommendation system.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.520
So we first exploit
our pre-processed deck

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:47.780
logs to extract
information about how

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:50.690
each characters are
associated, which

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:53.780
we call association rules.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:56.720
With this analysis, we
can score the strengths

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:02.330
of the relationship across
all the pair of characters.

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:05.920
If there is a request
from the player,

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:09.380
the recommendation
server response--

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:13.100
I mean, the received set of
characters in the player space

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:16.340
box, and it fills
all the 16 characters

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:21.350
according to the score
of association rules.

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:23.930
This function is very
flexible, and the player

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:27.740
can specify a deck archetype
and deck cost, and also

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:32.159
the favorite characters to
be included in the deck.

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:35.919
Regarding KPIs, we are
monitoring several metrics.

00:08:35.919 --> 00:08:39.780
For instance, we're
watching how many players

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.360
have used this
recommendation system,

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:46.260
and whether the recommended
deck has been accepted,

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:49.890
or whether the take contributed
to the players in terms

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:50.910
of win rate at battles.

00:08:53.810 --> 00:08:58.100
As for the Dojo, a player
can battle against AI

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:02.230
with various archetypes
and strengths.

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:04.980
So here, we train a
deep neural network

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:08.150
from pre-processed battle logs.

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:10.410
The client sent
current game status,

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:13.170
and the battle
API server returns

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:18.930
the inference of neural networks
at every turn of the battle.

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:21.480
And as for the metrics,
we're monitoring

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:25.260
how frequently the Dojo
is used for beginners,

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:29.130
and whether the strength of
AI was sufficient in terms

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:31.660
of win rate.

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:34.790
So these two AIs have
already been released,

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:39.120
and we are observing
possible KPIs.

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:41.770
And before showing
the results, I

00:09:41.770 --> 00:09:44.110
want to pass the talk
to Ikki, and he'll

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:46.870
talk about the technical
details, and some tips

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:49.620
for building AI.

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:50.910
IKKI TANAKA: Hi, everyone.

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:53.640
I'm Ikki, and a data
scientist at DeNA.

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:56.070
So I'm one of the
developers that

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:59.820
created our gaming system by
using machine learning and data

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:01.000
science.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.200
So today, I will talk of the
technical details of our use

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:06.330
cases with GCP.

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:10.320
So now let's look at the
story of the technical part.

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:15.630
First, I'd like to start off
by the deck recommendation.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:19.300
This is the outline of the
deck recommender algorithm.

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:22.020
Firstly, the algorithm
receives some inputs

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:25.720
from a player, such as a
deck archetype, the cost,

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:28.390
the possessed characters,
and the character

00:10:28.390 --> 00:10:30.580
the player wants to use.

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:32.970
Next, the algorithm
selects a leader

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:35.290
by using the result
of association

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:38.610
analysis and statistical data.

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:42.000
In this algorithm, the leader
character is chosen first,

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:47.790
and the next characters are
selected based on the leader.

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:51.330
The way associated
characters are simply

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:55.800
selected based on the result
of association analysis.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:59.030
If the deck is not
full of 16 characters,

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:02.880
another character is selected--

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:04.620
as a best character.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:07.590
After repeating this
procedure several times,

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:11.122
the recommended deck is
proposed to the player.

00:11:11.122 --> 00:11:13.920
If the game is different,
the way of fine tuning

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.253
is also different.

00:11:16.253 --> 00:11:17.670
In our game "Gyakuten
Othellonia,"

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:21.120
for example, we made additional
rules to decide a leader,

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.880
and we ignored the
characters whose skill will

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:25.860
be never activated in the deck.

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:31.370
I'd like to give a
brief explanation

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:33.740
about how to extract
the relationships

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:36.810
between characters.

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:40.820
In our recommendation, we are
using the association analysis.

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:44.520
The association analysis is a
rules-based machine-learning

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:47.430
method for discovering
meaningful relations

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:50.800
between content
in large data set.

00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:53.530
This method can perform
a high speed calculation

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:57.700
to extract the many rules,
and the extracted relations

00:11:57.700 --> 00:12:02.720
are easy to understand and
useful in various settings.

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:07.010
So our relations are
evaluated with some metrics,

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:09.420
like the strength
of the connections

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:13.340
and the frequency of
the rules and patterns.

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:16.220
Our recommendation with a deck
by selecting the characters

00:12:16.220 --> 00:12:20.770
sequentially is stronger
relationships one to another.

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:24.230
This method can handle the
various kinds of data easily,

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:28.180
so this technique is applicable
to many games and other fields.

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:34.300
Next, I will talk of the system.

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:39.430
So we build our applications,
like the deck recommendation

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:43.540
and the divining
AI bot with GCP.

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:47.870
So why did we choose GCP for
our machine learning systems?

00:12:47.870 --> 00:12:50.150
There are two main reasons.

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:53.920
The first line is that GCP has
powerful and flexible tools

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:57.330
for machine learning
and data science.

00:12:57.330 --> 00:13:00.870
In the data analysis, we often
try to investigate the data,

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:05.490
visualize it, and create
the prototype models.

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:08.640
The GCP services for
big data analysis

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:11.020
make it possible for
us, such as BigQuery,

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:14.910
acquire data lab, and
Google Compute Engine.

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:17.940
And cloud storage provides
scalable and faster storage

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:23.010
service for large
data, and it helps

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:30.250
us to manage big data and a
lot of machine learning models.

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:36.010
About Cloud ML Engine, we
can deploy the AI models

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:40.220
to Cloud Machine Learning
Engine easily and quickly,

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:44.730
and it also provides
user-friendly APIs.

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:50.750
Additionally, these people
buy many services to perform

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:53.400
machine learning efficiently.

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:57.900
For example, we are
trying to use a GK control

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.230
service and a Cloud
ML Engine training

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:02.230
with customer functions
for scalable [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:05.090
distributed
[INAUDIBLE] trainings.

00:14:05.090 --> 00:14:08.450
The second one is that GCP
has scalable and robust

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:11.370
infrastructure services.

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:13.320
We can run the
applications easily

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:17.100
by using Google App Engine with
several programming languages,

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:20.060
like Python and Go languages.

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:23.900
And we can check many metrics
through Stackdriver, logging,

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:25.700
and others.

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:29.770
I'd like to emphasize that
auto scaling of the Google App

00:14:29.770 --> 00:14:33.580
Engine is a very good service
because the new instances are

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:36.370
increased or stopped
in response to traffic,

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:41.995
and the requests are distributed
to the instances automatically.

00:14:41.995 --> 00:14:48.030
These GCP services are easy to
integrate into the same system.

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:51.020
Moreover, although I don't
touch on the product details,

00:14:51.020 --> 00:14:54.200
Google Cloud brings many
advantages to our business,

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:58.570
like a security
correlation and openness.

00:14:58.570 --> 00:15:00.610
For these many reasons,
we are choosing

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:02.065
GCP for our ML systems.

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:11.205
Next, I'll show the system
architecture of the deck

00:15:11.205 --> 00:15:13.590
recommendation with GCP.

00:15:13.590 --> 00:15:16.470
The deck logs are saved
in BigQuery, which

00:15:16.470 --> 00:15:19.300
are used to extract the rules.

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:22.170
We set the [INAUDIBLE] to
perform the batch processing

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:26.070
of the association analysis
in Google Compute Engine,

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:29.010
and in order to catch up the
trained decks with the latest

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:34.930
characters, we are performing
batch processing every day.

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:37.580
Then cloud functions
pre-processes the result

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:40.190
of association analysis
and updates the data

00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:43.560
for production
environment in GCS.

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:47.040
Cloud function checks if the
data is uploaded correctly,

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:50.030
for example, if the data
size is too small or large.

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:55.820
Then we are using
Google Engine Python

00:15:55.820 --> 00:16:00.970
3.7 for our
recommendation API server.

00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:04.020
The server receives a
request from the client,

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:06.900
then builds a deck
using analyzer data,

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:11.840
and finally, it returns a
recommended deck to the player.

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:14.410
One of the notable point
is that [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:16.850
Python 3.7 was
generally available

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:20.120
at the end of last year,
so we could use it just

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:22.260
at the right time.

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:25.830
As a result of
upgrade to Python 3.7,

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:28.920
we didn't need to change
our code that we had already

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:30.300
developed in Python 3.

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:35.950
So far, I've explained the
algorithm and the system

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:38.230
with deck recommendation.

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:41.040
Let's move on to the next
topic, deep learning AI bot.

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:43.880
It's called Othellonia Dojo.

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:46.620
Dojo means a training room
to improve playing skills.

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:52.850
This is an overview of the
training in deep learning AI

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:54.600
bot.

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:56.790
Deep learning was
suitable for our case

00:16:56.790 --> 00:17:00.240
to learn many complicated
features, such as the state

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:04.800
of decks, boards,
and hands, status,

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:08.430
and the combination
of the features.

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:12.420
The deep learning AI bot
receives a state of battle

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:13.900
from the client.

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:16.440
The information
battle is converted

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:20.670
to the numerical
features, which are used

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:24.230
as inputs of the deep learning.

00:17:24.230 --> 00:17:28.119
Here, we created
model 5000 features.

00:17:28.119 --> 00:17:31.910
The network learns the
actions of top-tier players

00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:34.890
as binary classification.

00:17:34.890 --> 00:17:37.380
Finally, the deep learning
model outputs the values

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:42.970
of each action that will be
used to decide the next action.

00:17:42.970 --> 00:17:45.530
We used more than 10 million
battle records in the training.

00:17:48.250 --> 00:17:50.440
In our training, we are
using the battle records

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:53.260
of highly skilled
players, but we

00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:56.560
need to create the AI
with appropriate skills

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:58.860
for beginners.

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:03.220
In order to divide the strengths
of AI into multiple levels,

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:06.150
we are processing the
inference results.

00:18:06.150 --> 00:18:09.480
Specifically, we select the
highest evaluation value

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:11.940
when we want to
make a strong AI,

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:15.470
and if we want to weaken
the strength of AI,

00:18:15.470 --> 00:18:20.800
we select the second or third
value with the probability.

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:23.530
Technically, the
probability is calculated

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:25.980
by [INAUDIBLE] function,
which transforms

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:30.650
the values into [INAUDIBLE]
property distribution.

00:18:30.650 --> 00:18:35.980
After tuning the probabilities,
we made five levels of AI.

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:38.440
In the latter part, we will
show the remarkable results

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:41.095
of the winning rates
against the actual players.

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:48.250
Next, I will show
the system of AI bot.

00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:52.390
First of all, I'll talk
about the training phase.

00:18:52.390 --> 00:18:54.445
A large number of
battle records in PvP

00:18:54.445 --> 00:18:59.250
is stored in cloud
storage through BigQuery.

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:01.980
The training process consists
of the pre-processing

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:04.200
of the features
and model training,

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:07.580
which are running in
Google Compute Engine.

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:09.840
At the pre-processing,
the features

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:15.140
are generated on demand
by emulating the game.

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:17.190
In our case, we created
the custom function

00:19:17.190 --> 00:19:19.610
of data generator that
creates the features

00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:21.960
during the training.

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:24.150
After the training,
the trained AI model

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:27.740
is saved in cloud storage
to deploy onto the Cloud ML

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:29.900
engine.

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:32.270
Regarding the continuous
integration and delivery

00:19:32.270 --> 00:19:34.250
of machine learning--

00:19:34.250 --> 00:19:38.930
it's so-called CI/CD-- we are
testing the training modules

00:19:38.930 --> 00:19:40.910
to check the dependencies
and the features

00:19:40.910 --> 00:19:46.280
automatically by using the unit
test and the evaluation test.

00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:51.260
Also, it is important to deploy
the AI model automatically

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:54.120
in short cycles with
high reliability

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:57.880
when releasing new models.

00:19:57.880 --> 00:19:59.370
We are currently
trying to simplify

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:04.320
the complicated process from
training to deployment by using

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:09.540
CI/CD the tools for executing
it more quickly and easy.

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:11.430
I think we can
leverage the cloud

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:15.170
to build the continuous
delivery service of GCP

00:20:15.170 --> 00:20:16.550
to accomplish better CI/CD.

00:20:20.510 --> 00:20:23.050
I'll move on to the
inference phase.

00:20:23.050 --> 00:20:24.940
Please take a look
at the communication

00:20:24.940 --> 00:20:27.850
between the client and
the inference API server

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:30.072
on the left of the slide.

00:20:30.072 --> 00:20:32.280
The battle record is a
[INAUDIBLE] design [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:37.435
API server from the client who
is playing against an AI bot.

00:20:37.435 --> 00:20:40.250
The inference API server
reproduces a battle

00:20:40.250 --> 00:20:43.530
from the record, then
extracts the features,

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:46.020
and it sends the features
to the Online Prediction

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:49.220
API of the Cloud ML Engine.

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:51.090
Online Prediction
of Cloud ML Engine

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:54.090
is very useful because
it is optimized

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:58.650
to run the AI model with
low latency in real time.

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:01.150
Cloud ML engine uses
a trained AI model

00:21:01.150 --> 00:21:03.820
stored in cloud
storage, and returns

00:21:03.820 --> 00:21:06.580
the values of each action.

00:21:06.580 --> 00:21:08.980
The values are sent
back to the client,

00:21:08.980 --> 00:21:11.380
and the client moves
towards the battle step

00:21:11.380 --> 00:21:16.050
by selecting the best action
according to the values.

00:21:16.050 --> 00:21:20.350
That's the overall communication
between the client and server.

00:21:20.350 --> 00:21:22.420
[INAUDIBLE] engine
and Cloud ML Engine

00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:25.900
can auto scale in
response to the traffic.

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:28.060
That's a very good function.

00:21:28.060 --> 00:21:30.970
By taking the benefit
of the autoscaling,

00:21:30.970 --> 00:21:32.790
we can manage a large
amount of requests.

00:21:36.630 --> 00:21:38.950
Lastly, I'll touch on
the technical topic--

00:21:38.950 --> 00:21:41.790
the testing of the systems.

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:44.930
So why should we
test the systems?

00:21:44.930 --> 00:21:47.930
I mentioned in the last
slide the Cloud ML engine

00:21:47.930 --> 00:21:49.160
can autoscale.

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:53.150
That's good-- yes,
that's true, but we

00:21:53.150 --> 00:21:56.150
didn't have enough knowledge
about how much load

00:21:56.150 --> 00:21:58.930
the system can withstand.

00:21:58.930 --> 00:22:01.230
In the mobile game,
generally the load

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:06.220
tends to increase at the
moment a new event opens.

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:08.295
The figure on the
slide shows the example

00:22:08.295 --> 00:22:14.790
of the estimated requests per
second in a realistic event.

00:22:14.790 --> 00:22:17.720
This is a trial calculation
that the request per second will

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:21.620
increased to several
hundreds in 10 minutes.

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:24.080
We have to check in
advance if is a system

00:22:24.080 --> 00:22:27.590
can withstand like this load.

00:22:27.590 --> 00:22:33.080
As a point-- we cannot predict
the action of AI as batch

00:22:33.080 --> 00:22:37.310
processing at regular intervals.

00:22:37.310 --> 00:22:41.290
Our system has to respond to
the request in real time--

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:46.150
that is, where the should put
the character in the next turn.

00:22:46.150 --> 00:22:48.860
To achieve this, we chose
the online prediction

00:22:48.860 --> 00:22:50.110
of the Cloud ML Engine.

00:22:55.080 --> 00:22:57.880
We have checked if the
Cloud ML Engine can satisfy

00:22:57.880 --> 00:23:01.370
our requirements by
performing a lot of testings

00:23:01.370 --> 00:23:03.980
in advance of the release.

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:06.440
In our case, the
testing was performed

00:23:06.440 --> 00:23:08.960
in the setting of the
request to increase linearly

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:13.310
and to reach a maximum
1000 request per second.

00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:16.330
We found that the Cloud ML
engine can scale quickly

00:23:16.330 --> 00:23:19.270
in response to the requests,
and the average latency

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:23.110
of the prediction is
less than one second.

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:24.940
However, there were
[INAUDIBLE] that

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:28.960
took a longer time than
usual, since this might not

00:23:28.960 --> 00:23:34.910
be possible to return all the
request to the player quickly,

00:23:34.910 --> 00:23:39.330
so we needed to
resolve this condition.

00:23:39.330 --> 00:23:41.280
Our team and the
Cloud ML Engine team

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:45.480
cooperated to investigate
the bottlenecks.

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:50.220
Then we found that the
timeout had happened

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:53.040
when the new instances
warmed up in the process

00:23:53.040 --> 00:23:56.880
of the autoscaling
due to our heavy load.

00:23:56.880 --> 00:24:00.300
It was challenging to handle a
large number of requests which

00:24:00.300 --> 00:24:03.390
consist of many features
at the same time

00:24:03.390 --> 00:24:05.880
with online prediction.

00:24:05.880 --> 00:24:07.850
But if we can
resolve this problem

00:24:07.850 --> 00:24:11.070
by building an efficient
back end system,

00:24:11.070 --> 00:24:13.670
we don't have to warm up
the system beforehand,

00:24:13.670 --> 00:24:17.160
and the system will
be more robust.

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:19.920
So our team took the time
to deal with this problem.

00:24:23.310 --> 00:24:28.040
Let's take a look at how we
tackle to remove the errors.

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:30.270
When the error returns
within the time,

00:24:30.270 --> 00:24:34.070
we can simply throw it
back again to the API.

00:24:34.070 --> 00:24:39.430
But in the case of timeout,
the system cannot send it back

00:24:39.430 --> 00:24:43.320
again because a timeout
error is not returned.

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:46.050
Therefore when the inference
API server does not

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:49.680
receive the response
within five seconds,

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:51.840
the request is sent
back to the API

00:24:51.840 --> 00:24:56.680
again, because something bad
has happened in the inference.

00:24:56.680 --> 00:25:00.790
Also, if the destination of the
request is the same endpoint,

00:25:00.790 --> 00:25:04.370
it will further increase
the load on the server.

00:25:04.370 --> 00:25:07.250
As a result, we set
another backup endpoint

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:09.890
to receive the request
that fails to be

00:25:09.890 --> 00:25:12.880
operated in the main endpoint.

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:17.230
That is the second request sent
to the backup endpoint, which

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:19.450
is prepared independently
from the main endpoint.

00:25:23.500 --> 00:25:25.420
This is a result of
a lot of testings

00:25:25.420 --> 00:25:28.420
with a backup endpoint.

00:25:28.420 --> 00:25:31.240
The figure shows
the total error rate

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:35.320
of several settings-- no backup,
backup with 10 minimum nodes,

00:25:35.320 --> 00:25:38.770
and the backup with
20 minimum nodes.

00:25:38.770 --> 00:25:42.500
In the case without backup
endpoint, on the left,

00:25:42.500 --> 00:25:46.950
about 1.3% of errors remained.

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:50.390
In the middle setting of the
backup with 10 minimum nodes,

00:25:50.390 --> 00:25:52.540
although small errors
still remained,

00:25:52.540 --> 00:25:57.160
the backup can deal with
mostly the main point errors.

00:25:57.160 --> 00:25:59.360
We found that the reason
of this small error

00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:02.930
was because the number of
backup instances was small,

00:26:02.930 --> 00:26:05.050
so the timeout
had still happened

00:26:05.050 --> 00:26:10.680
as a warmup of the autoscaling
in the backup environment.

00:26:10.680 --> 00:26:13.430
The rightmost case is
a fine tuned setting,

00:26:13.430 --> 00:26:15.070
where the number
of backup instances

00:26:15.070 --> 00:26:20.170
were set to 20 for covering
the main endpoint errors

00:26:20.170 --> 00:26:22.100
sufficiently.

00:26:22.100 --> 00:26:24.710
In this case, we could
handle all the requests

00:26:24.710 --> 00:26:27.330
without errors.

00:26:27.330 --> 00:26:30.210
In this way, DeNA and
the Cloud ML Engine team

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:34.590
got over the challenge high and
spiky traffic and the latest

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:36.880
limit without errors.

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:38.850
After deployment
in this setting,

00:26:38.850 --> 00:26:42.325
the system has been stable
in handling many requests.

00:26:45.790 --> 00:26:47.200
Let me show you one more thing--

00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:52.210
it's a useful service to
monitor the system performances.

00:26:52.210 --> 00:26:53.940
We can check the
server performances

00:26:53.940 --> 00:26:57.790
through Stackdriver, which
provides error reporting, auto

00:26:57.790 --> 00:27:02.790
notification, logging, trace
of latency, and many others.

00:27:02.790 --> 00:27:07.140
Stackdriver dashboard especially
provides a visualization too

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:11.480
that is easy to add a
chart and customize it.

00:27:11.480 --> 00:27:14.150
We are monitoring the
many metrics according

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:16.740
to our needs in one place.

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:18.290
For example, the
number of requests,

00:27:18.290 --> 00:27:20.870
API latencies, and
CPU and memory usage,

00:27:20.870 --> 00:27:25.757
and the number of learning
instances in real time.

00:27:25.757 --> 00:27:27.590
Additionally, you can
change the time window

00:27:27.590 --> 00:27:31.890
to focus on and check
the percent of values.

00:27:31.890 --> 00:27:35.190
We are using 99 percentile
values for anomaly detection

00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:37.830
in the system.

00:27:37.830 --> 00:27:43.590
So far, I have explained the
technical details of our AI

00:27:43.590 --> 00:27:44.820
with GCP.

00:27:44.820 --> 00:27:48.000
The system with GCP can
be constructed flexibly

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:52.650
to cope with various problems
in gaming and other fields.

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:56.260
Now I'd like to hand over to Jun
for the KPI result of our use

00:27:56.260 --> 00:27:58.220
cases in the future.

00:27:58.220 --> 00:27:58.720
Thanks.

00:28:06.663 --> 00:28:07.330
JUN OKUMURA: OK.

00:28:07.330 --> 00:28:10.670
So this is our result part.

00:28:10.670 --> 00:28:14.500
So as Ikki mentions, we needed
a scalable and robust system

00:28:14.500 --> 00:28:17.500
to successfully reduce our AIs.

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:19.330
So in this final
part, I would like

00:28:19.330 --> 00:28:23.380
to share what happened
after the release.

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:26.800
So first, for the
deck recommendation,

00:28:26.800 --> 00:28:30.220
here is a chart which shows
the number of beginners

00:28:30.220 --> 00:28:32.560
who use deck recommendations.

00:28:32.560 --> 00:28:35.090
There were constant
usage after the release,

00:28:35.090 --> 00:28:39.870
but this function was not
well known at this time.

00:28:39.870 --> 00:28:42.780
But the station has
been changed after we

00:28:42.780 --> 00:28:45.240
started an in-game
event to promote

00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:48.150
players to build new decks.

00:28:48.150 --> 00:28:50.040
So the deck
recommendation system

00:28:50.040 --> 00:28:53.910
has been recognized more,
and even after the event

00:28:53.910 --> 00:28:58.050
has finished, many players,
and mostly beginners,

00:28:58.050 --> 00:29:02.470
are still using these
function frequently.

00:29:02.470 --> 00:29:06.520
So second, I will [INAUDIBLE]
that the recommended deck

00:29:06.520 --> 00:29:10.540
push the win rates of
the beginners by about 5%

00:29:10.540 --> 00:29:15.770
compared to deck created
without that recommendation.

00:29:15.770 --> 00:29:19.240
So since our focus was
to support beginners,

00:29:19.240 --> 00:29:23.290
we are very satisfied
with these results.

00:29:23.290 --> 00:29:26.450
Also, I'd like to mention
that this function has

00:29:26.450 --> 00:29:32.020
good adoption, even
from top tier players.

00:29:32.020 --> 00:29:34.390
This was a sort of
byproduct, but we

00:29:34.390 --> 00:29:37.570
are observing that
heavy players usually

00:29:37.570 --> 00:29:41.710
update their deck frequently,
and this recommendation system

00:29:41.710 --> 00:29:45.580
mitigates the course of
piecing deck from scratch.

00:29:45.580 --> 00:29:48.850
They use recommendation
as a first suggestion,

00:29:48.850 --> 00:29:53.670
and then customize
the deck as they want.

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:57.710
So let's see the
system matrix as well.

00:29:57.710 --> 00:30:00.920
After the release, we
have a stable latency

00:30:00.920 --> 00:30:04.890
of 20 millisecond, which is
fast enough for our cases,

00:30:04.890 --> 00:30:08.700
and we're surprised by the
result, because recommend

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:10.940
has shown our rhythm
is very complex,

00:30:10.940 --> 00:30:16.070
and we haven't expected
such a low latency.

00:30:16.070 --> 00:30:18.440
So the autoscaling
does a good job,

00:30:18.440 --> 00:30:21.230
and we haven't had any
system trouble so far.

00:30:24.260 --> 00:30:27.170
So briefly, we will explain
about the evaluation

00:30:27.170 --> 00:30:30.050
of the deep learning AI bot.

00:30:30.050 --> 00:30:32.150
After the release,
we are observing

00:30:32.150 --> 00:30:37.070
that our AI bot has good
enough win rate as a practice

00:30:37.070 --> 00:30:39.800
partner for beginners.

00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:45.560
So this chart shows the win
rates of AI against players.

00:30:45.560 --> 00:30:49.490
The horizontal axis denotes
the player's skill levels,

00:30:49.490 --> 00:30:55.160
and the vertical axis
is the win rate of AI.

00:30:55.160 --> 00:31:00.290
So in the Dojo, we provide
five different AI levels,

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:03.740
and the strongest one can
even beat top-tier players

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:06.150
with a win rate of 50%.

00:31:06.150 --> 00:31:08.990
So we think that our
AI is strong enough

00:31:08.990 --> 00:31:09.890
for beginner support.

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:17.220
So from beginner's perspective,
win rates of AI ranges

00:31:17.220 --> 00:31:21.720
from about 20% to 8%, which
means that the player can

00:31:21.720 --> 00:31:23.380
choose any--

00:31:23.380 --> 00:31:25.680
I mean appropriate--
levels of AI

00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:29.100
according to their
skills or preferences.

00:31:29.100 --> 00:31:35.130
So therefore, this AI works
as a sort of a good mentor.

00:31:35.130 --> 00:31:37.350
Although this was
released last month,

00:31:37.350 --> 00:31:41.280
and we don't have enough data
to evaluate the business impact,

00:31:41.280 --> 00:31:44.100
those rates are used
by beginners very well,

00:31:44.100 --> 00:31:49.000
and we hope that these can lead
to a good KPI in the future.

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:53.060
And as for the system
metrics, the inference

00:31:53.060 --> 00:31:57.360
of this Cloud Machine Learning
Engine is about 200 to 300

00:31:57.360 --> 00:32:00.100
millisecond, and very stable.

00:32:00.100 --> 00:32:03.010
Even for the entire
system, the response time

00:32:03.010 --> 00:32:07.110
is about a second, which is
enough fast for our case.

00:32:07.110 --> 00:32:09.970
And backup endpoint
is working well,

00:32:09.970 --> 00:32:14.750
and we don't have
system trouble so far.

00:32:14.750 --> 00:32:18.570
So at the end of my
talk, I'll show items

00:32:18.570 --> 00:32:21.680
which worked in our cases.

00:32:21.680 --> 00:32:26.750
So first, to train the AI that
play the game like a human,

00:32:26.750 --> 00:32:30.620
we need the battle logs that
completely reproduce battle

00:32:30.620 --> 00:32:32.670
status and actions.

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:36.270
So if you have some random
effects in the game,

00:32:36.270 --> 00:32:40.340
make sure that the random
seeds are stored in the logs.

00:32:40.340 --> 00:32:44.650
So secondly, for the
evolution of our AI,

00:32:44.650 --> 00:32:46.810
we needed to run
thousands of battles

00:32:46.810 --> 00:32:50.980
to get performance statistics,
so a [INAUDIBLE] simulator

00:32:50.980 --> 00:32:54.070
helps us a lot in this context.

00:32:54.070 --> 00:32:57.910
And, well, if you want to try
to use reinforcement learning

00:32:57.910 --> 00:33:01.800
techniques, you also need to--

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:06.130
a very, very fast simulator,
because the number

00:33:06.130 --> 00:33:09.730
of the battle that our
[INAUDIBLE] goes through really

00:33:09.730 --> 00:33:12.130
matters.

00:33:12.130 --> 00:33:14.620
And the third, we
also want to stress

00:33:14.620 --> 00:33:17.020
that AI scalable
and robust system

00:33:17.020 --> 00:33:22.570
is critical for implementing
AI in real services.

00:33:22.570 --> 00:33:25.780
We think that GCP in particular,
[INAUDIBLE] Machine Learning

00:33:25.780 --> 00:33:29.960
Engine, and GAE were
a good choice for us.

00:33:29.960 --> 00:33:34.730
And as for the CI/CD, we have
to think about how frequently,

00:33:34.730 --> 00:33:39.410
or by which trigger, the
AI model should be updated.

00:33:39.410 --> 00:33:43.010
Because the game environment
changes frequently,

00:33:43.010 --> 00:33:45.260
we need to keep--

00:33:45.260 --> 00:33:51.070
adding features and retraining
the models after [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:51.070 --> 00:33:53.680
So this operation is
very heavy, and we

00:33:53.680 --> 00:33:57.970
have to think about the CI/CD,
I mean before the release,

00:33:57.970 --> 00:34:02.230
in particular in the
AI modeling phase.

00:34:02.230 --> 00:34:05.980
The final item is not
technical, but a critical.

00:34:05.980 --> 00:34:09.670
Generally speaking, we don't
know how well AI will perform,

00:34:09.670 --> 00:34:12.730
and how big the
business impact will

00:34:12.730 --> 00:34:15.880
be before starting a project.

00:34:15.880 --> 00:34:18.610
So in such an uncertain case--

00:34:18.610 --> 00:34:22.270
situation-- the communication
and controlling the expectation

00:34:22.270 --> 00:34:24.350
is very, very important.

00:34:24.350 --> 00:34:26.949
Although we are machine
learning engineers,

00:34:26.949 --> 00:34:29.770
we try to understand the
game and the business issues

00:34:29.770 --> 00:34:33.580
correctly, and I think
that this was a key factor

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:36.219
of the success of this project.

00:34:36.219 --> 00:34:38.889
So that's all from
me, and I'd like

00:34:38.889 --> 00:34:41.230
to pass the summary to Henry.

00:34:41.230 --> 00:34:43.940
Thanks.

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:47.230
[SIDE CONVERSATION]

00:34:47.893 --> 00:34:49.060
HENRY TAPPEN: Hi, everybody.

00:34:49.060 --> 00:34:49.810
I'm Henry Tappen--

00:34:49.810 --> 00:34:52.627
I'm a product manager on
the AI platform team working

00:34:52.627 --> 00:34:54.960
on what was formerly known
as the Cloud Machine Learning

00:34:54.960 --> 00:34:59.050
Engine, but we've just
rebranded to be the AI Platform.

00:34:59.050 --> 00:35:00.482
This was a really
exciting thing,

00:35:00.482 --> 00:35:02.190
so we've been working
with the DeNA folks

00:35:02.190 --> 00:35:04.950
for a while for this one,
mostly being inspired

00:35:04.950 --> 00:35:08.880
by the very elegant way that
they took a number of machine

00:35:08.880 --> 00:35:11.790
learning techniques and
built an awesome experience

00:35:11.790 --> 00:35:14.010
for their beginner gamers.

00:35:14.010 --> 00:35:16.225
Now when we saw
this, we notice there

00:35:16.225 --> 00:35:17.850
was a lot of different
things that they

00:35:17.850 --> 00:35:20.220
had to use a lot of different
pieces of technology

00:35:20.220 --> 00:35:23.400
between App Engine, Compute
Engine, the Cloud Machine

00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:25.870
Learning Engine, and
so on and so forth.

00:35:25.870 --> 00:35:27.510
So the challenge
to us at Google is,

00:35:27.510 --> 00:35:30.810
how could we make it
even easier for teams

00:35:30.810 --> 00:35:32.790
like DeNA, or other
gaming companies,

00:35:32.790 --> 00:35:35.220
to get started even
faster producing

00:35:35.220 --> 00:35:38.220
these kinds of innovative,
but also very accessible,

00:35:38.220 --> 00:35:40.260
gaming experiences
for it using things

00:35:40.260 --> 00:35:42.750
like machine learning and AI?

00:35:42.750 --> 00:35:44.670
So we've been working
on a number of features

00:35:44.670 --> 00:35:48.360
to make the process of
getting started that much

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:49.733
easier and that much simpler.

00:35:49.733 --> 00:35:51.150
So one of the ones
that we noticed

00:35:51.150 --> 00:35:53.190
was starting to think
more about machine

00:35:53.190 --> 00:35:57.450
learning beyond just
TensorFlow, scikit, and XGBoost.

00:35:57.450 --> 00:35:58.950
So Cloud Machine
Learning Engine was

00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:00.810
one of the first
cloud-based platforms

00:36:00.810 --> 00:36:04.360
that enabled you to train a
TensorFlow model in the cloud.

00:36:04.360 --> 00:36:06.640
Since then, in the
last few years,

00:36:06.640 --> 00:36:09.060
we've added support
for other libraries,

00:36:09.060 --> 00:36:11.700
like scikit-learn and XGBoost.

00:36:11.700 --> 00:36:13.950
But especially,
seeing what was done

00:36:13.950 --> 00:36:17.790
with association rules and
other data mining techniques,

00:36:17.790 --> 00:36:20.283
we realized that the world--

00:36:20.283 --> 00:36:22.200
and the world and the
problems are much bigger

00:36:22.200 --> 00:36:25.510
than that, which is easy to
do inside of these frameworks.

00:36:25.510 --> 00:36:27.210
And so we've been
working on introducing

00:36:27.210 --> 00:36:30.570
support for custom containers
into our training component.

00:36:30.570 --> 00:36:32.580
This is now available
in a public beta--

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:36.240
we expect to go to full release,
probably in this quarter,

00:36:36.240 --> 00:36:38.270
but certainly inside of 2019.

00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:41.190
Where with custom containers,
you can take any code,

00:36:41.190 --> 00:36:44.010
any framework that you can stick
inside of a Docker container,

00:36:44.010 --> 00:36:46.110
and then push it to the
Cloud Machine Learning

00:36:46.110 --> 00:36:48.300
Engine in the same
way that you would

00:36:48.300 --> 00:36:51.060
use TensorFlow models
or scikit or XGBoost

00:36:51.060 --> 00:36:52.980
or any other kinds
of training scripts.

00:36:52.980 --> 00:36:55.950
So this gives you access to,
not only traditional machine

00:36:55.950 --> 00:36:58.610
learning frameworks like
TensorFlow, PyTorch, Cafe, et

00:36:58.610 --> 00:37:00.877
cetera, but also to
run any other software

00:37:00.877 --> 00:37:01.710
that you might need.

00:37:01.710 --> 00:37:04.510
So if you've written
some C++, some Java code,

00:37:04.510 --> 00:37:09.150
some different Python routines,
even our code for solving

00:37:09.150 --> 00:37:13.330
certain parts of your analysis
or production of models,

00:37:13.330 --> 00:37:16.643
you can now go ahead and easily
train them on the Cloud Machine

00:37:16.643 --> 00:37:17.310
Learning Engine.

00:37:17.310 --> 00:37:19.230
Additionally, it's
not shown on here,

00:37:19.230 --> 00:37:21.120
but we've also been
thinking more about

00:37:21.120 --> 00:37:24.900
how we get easier pipelines for
solving some of these things.

00:37:24.900 --> 00:37:29.490
So earlier-- late last year, we
released into the open source

00:37:29.490 --> 00:37:32.160
a system called Kubeflow
Pipelines, which

00:37:32.160 --> 00:37:34.380
is a composition
and an orchestration

00:37:34.380 --> 00:37:38.640
engine for running machine
learning-focused workloads.

00:37:38.640 --> 00:37:42.270
On Kubeflow Pipelines, you can
start up a Kubernetes cluster

00:37:42.270 --> 00:37:45.330
on GKE, or on your own
premises, et cetera;

00:37:45.330 --> 00:37:48.600
specify a simple workflow that
says all the different steps

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:50.370
you'd like to do on
the way, including

00:37:50.370 --> 00:37:52.950
training jobs on the Cloud
Machine Learning Engine;

00:37:52.950 --> 00:37:54.810
and then the Kubeflow
Pipeline system

00:37:54.810 --> 00:37:58.140
will take care of doing
all the steps necessary,

00:37:58.140 --> 00:38:00.360
making sure that it
runs, runs effectively.

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:02.973
So this could be a
great way for tackling

00:38:02.973 --> 00:38:04.890
bigger parts of your
machine learning workflow

00:38:04.890 --> 00:38:08.465
besides just the actual training
part at the very end of it.

00:38:08.465 --> 00:38:10.590
So the second thing that
we really were inspired by

00:38:10.590 --> 00:38:13.020
and have been working on is--

00:38:13.020 --> 00:38:17.240
I think Jun-san pointed out
very, very well that when--

00:38:17.240 --> 00:38:21.405
and Ikki as well-- that when
predictions come back from--

00:38:24.750 --> 00:38:27.750
the machine learning framework
or as you're putting data

00:38:27.750 --> 00:38:30.972
into the systems, you often
need to do other transformations

00:38:30.972 --> 00:38:32.430
to make it compatible
for the game.

00:38:32.430 --> 00:38:34.920
So coming back to the
example of the AI,

00:38:34.920 --> 00:38:39.150
it wasn't enough to just
get a particular score back

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:42.780
from the model, but they
also needed to select,

00:38:42.780 --> 00:38:45.300
based on the player's skill
level, a random choice

00:38:45.300 --> 00:38:47.150
of a different option in it.

00:38:47.150 --> 00:38:49.200
Now this could be
really hard, and you

00:38:49.200 --> 00:38:52.350
might have to set up different
servers for post-processing

00:38:52.350 --> 00:38:54.420
or pre-processing the
data before you send it

00:38:54.420 --> 00:38:56.010
to the Machine Learning Engine.

00:38:56.010 --> 00:38:58.980
So to make this simpler,
we have available as

00:38:58.980 --> 00:39:02.190
of today in beta the
ability to run any Python

00:39:02.190 --> 00:39:05.730
code on the Cloud Machine
Learning Engine prediction

00:39:05.730 --> 00:39:09.070
service before or after
you make any predictions.

00:39:09.070 --> 00:39:11.520
So if you need to
reshape some data,

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:14.310
maybe take a different result,
convert from a score back

00:39:14.310 --> 00:39:17.130
into label, so on and
so forth, it's now just

00:39:17.130 --> 00:39:19.410
as simple as giving
us a Python class,

00:39:19.410 --> 00:39:22.343
and you've got whatever you need
to make happen along the way.

00:39:22.343 --> 00:39:23.760
This, incidentally,
also gives you

00:39:23.760 --> 00:39:25.560
some more flexibility as well.

00:39:25.560 --> 00:39:28.080
We allow you to install
any Python packages

00:39:28.080 --> 00:39:30.837
on the inference server
as well, and then

00:39:30.837 --> 00:39:32.920
do whatever you'd like
with inside those packages.

00:39:32.920 --> 00:39:37.170
So you have many more
options for running jobs

00:39:37.170 --> 00:39:39.940
on side of our machine
learning systems.

00:39:39.940 --> 00:39:41.820
And then finally, the
other big advancement

00:39:41.820 --> 00:39:44.743
that we've seen that was I think
especially relevant for gaming,

00:39:44.743 --> 00:39:46.410
and one thing that
was inspired by DeNA,

00:39:46.410 --> 00:39:50.850
is giving better flexibility
and better capacity for it.

00:39:50.850 --> 00:39:54.060
So you know, it's
really great to see

00:39:54.060 --> 00:39:55.710
heavy usage of our
systems, and we

00:39:55.710 --> 00:39:57.480
want to make sure
that we're giving you

00:39:57.480 --> 00:40:00.060
exactly the capabilities
that you need,

00:40:00.060 --> 00:40:02.490
that you're able to run
your jobs bulletproof,

00:40:02.490 --> 00:40:05.010
even for when you
have high demand,

00:40:05.010 --> 00:40:06.600
burst-y instances for it.

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:09.480
So today, in the
Machine Learning Engine

00:40:09.480 --> 00:40:12.780
on the prediction side,
you're able to choose

00:40:12.780 --> 00:40:16.110
either a four-core or
a dual-core machine

00:40:16.110 --> 00:40:17.940
with a choice of RAM in there.

00:40:17.940 --> 00:40:21.570
We're expanding this range of
options to be virtually any

00:40:21.570 --> 00:40:24.223
machine type that's supported on
Cloud Machine Learning Engine,

00:40:24.223 --> 00:40:26.640
or-- we're sorry, that's for
support on the Google Compute

00:40:26.640 --> 00:40:27.420
Engine--

00:40:27.420 --> 00:40:32.390
and including into the support
for accelerators like GPUs.

00:40:32.390 --> 00:40:36.890
So that way, if you've got a job
that is very intense, or very

00:40:36.890 --> 00:40:40.310
burst-y, or you want to maintain
a certain level of latency,

00:40:40.310 --> 00:40:43.250
you're now able to select
many more machine types

00:40:43.250 --> 00:40:44.780
and much more power
to those ones,

00:40:44.780 --> 00:40:47.210
especially those we're looking
for really complex models,

00:40:47.210 --> 00:40:49.010
like reinforcement
learning, where you're

00:40:49.010 --> 00:40:51.050
trying to beat the
next StarCraft AI,

00:40:51.050 --> 00:40:54.270
this might be a
great option for you.

00:40:54.270 --> 00:40:56.100
So let's kind of sum
up with these things.

00:40:56.100 --> 00:40:59.090
This was a chance
for us to see that AI

00:40:59.090 --> 00:41:03.295
can be used for much more than
recognizing images or speech

00:41:03.295 --> 00:41:04.670
or something, but
we can actually

00:41:04.670 --> 00:41:07.730
use machine learning AI to
create compelling gaming

00:41:07.730 --> 00:41:08.900
experiences.

00:41:08.900 --> 00:41:11.780
Both to challenge
our players, for when

00:41:11.780 --> 00:41:13.910
we want to give
them a smarter AI,

00:41:13.910 --> 00:41:17.390
but also to assist them so
that beginners have an even

00:41:17.390 --> 00:41:18.860
playing field with
those who have

00:41:18.860 --> 00:41:20.297
been on it for a long time.

00:41:20.297 --> 00:41:22.130
And the result of these
kinds of experiences

00:41:22.130 --> 00:41:25.225
can result in better engagement
and revenue for your game.

00:41:25.225 --> 00:41:27.350
And then finally, to note
that Google Cloud is here

00:41:27.350 --> 00:41:29.600
to help-- we're very interested
in the gaming segment,

00:41:29.600 --> 00:41:31.920
we're very interested
in machine learning.

00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:35.180
So anything that we can do
to help you achieve success

00:41:35.180 --> 00:41:37.780
in your gaming,
we're happy to do it.

